21
00:01:59.529 --> 00:02:05.353
NIST's incident-response recommendations
integrate preparation and response with

22
00:02:05.353 --> 00:02:11.176
broader cybersecurity risk management; use
that discipline to make roles,

23
00:02:11.176 --> 00:02:17.000
evidence, decisions, and recovery
expectations ready before an emergency.

24
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.538
Consider a synthetic incident after a
model rollout. Queue delay

25
00:02:22.538 --> 00:02:28.077
rises, one replica reports the candidate
alias but an old

26
00:02:28.077 --> 00:02:33.615
artifact digest, and a user says an action
timed out.

27
00:02:33.615 --> 00:02:39.154
Confirmed facts are limited: queue delay
exceeded the objective, identity

28
00:02:39.154 --> 00:02:44.692
is inconsistent, and the client did not
receive completion. It

29
00:02:44.692 --> 00:02:50.231
is not yet confirmed whether the external
action occurred or

30
00:02:50.231 --> 00:02:55.769
whether the weights caused the delay.
Severity accounts for the

31
00:02:55.769 --> 00:03:01.308
credible duplicate-action and
authorization impact, not merely the

32
00:03:01.308 --> 00:03:06.846
visible latency. The incident lead assigns
an evidence owner, serving

33
00:03:06.846 --> 00:03:12.385
responder, application responder,
communications owner, and rollback

34
00:03:12.385 --> 00:03:17.923
authority. The first update states the
time, affected route, current

35
00:03:17.923 --> 00:03:23.462
uncertainty, bounded containment, service
status, and next decision. This prevents

36
00:03:23.462 --> 00:03:29.000
a fluent but unsupported root-cause story
from replacing the investigation.

37
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:35.400
Contain at the narrowest boundary that
reliably stops ongoing impact.

38
00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:41.800
Reject one workload, disable a route or
tool, revoke a

39
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:48.200
principal or secret, quarantine an
artifact, stop a rollout, reduce

40
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:54.600
concurrency, isolate a node or site,
require manual approval, or

41
00:03:54.600 --> 00:04:01.000
switch to a separately evaluated fallback.
Keep service unavailable when

42
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:07.400
no safe mode exists. Prevent automatic
retries from multiplying load

43
00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:13.800
or repeating uncertain side effects.
Preserve evidence without creating another

44
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:20.200
incident. Capture necessary timestamps,
bounded correlation identifiers, exact

45
00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:26.600
release and artifact identities, digests,
policy decisions, redacted logs, metrics,

46
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:33.000
traces, queue state, deployment history,
approvals, configuration changes, and

47
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:39.400
observed postconditions. Follow retention,
privacy, legal-hold, and access policy. An

48
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:45.800
incident is not permission to collect
every prompt, response, personal

49
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:52.200
identifier, credential, secret, or hidden
reasoning. OpenTelemetry conventions can

50
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:58.600
help correlate signals, but your
application-owned contract and evidence

51
00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:05.000
policy determine which fields are
justified and safe.

52
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:11.333
Diagnose the first failed boundary with
evidence. Test artifact integrity

53
00:05:11.333 --> 00:05:17.667
and provenance; runtime, driver, and
library compatibility; endpoint and

54
00:05:17.667 --> 00:05:24.000
adapter contracts; identity and object or
function authorization; network and

55
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:30.333
secret access; evaluation behavior;
prompt, retrieval, and data inputs;

56
00:05:30.333 --> 00:05:36.667
capacity, placement, and queues;
dependencies; telemetry; and recent change

57
00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:43.000
history. More than one boundary can fail.
A bad release

58
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:49.333
may create memory pressure, which creates
queueing, which triggers unsafe

59
00:05:49.333 --> 00:05:55.667
retries, while an identity mismatch makes
attribution harder. Classify evidence

60
00:05:55.667 --> 00:06:02.000
as transient, deterministic, policy,
authorization, corrupt-state, capacity,

61
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:08.333
dependency, telemetry, or
incompatible-release failure because each

62
00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:14.667
permits different recovery. For any
unknown external or user-visible outcome,

63
00:06:14.667 --> 00:06:21.000
query the authoritative system of record
and observed postcondition before

64
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:27.333
retrying, compensating, or declaring
success. A client timeout proves that

65
00:06:27.333 --> 00:06:33.667
the client did not receive completion; it
does not prove

66
00:06:33.667 --> 00:06:40.000
that downstream work did not happen.

67
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:46.750
Checkpoint. A tool call times out after
the request left

68
00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:53.500
the model service. The user sees an error,
but the

69
00:06:53.500 --> 00:07:00.250
external system may have completed the
action. Should the service

70
00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:07.000
retry, and what evidence must come first?

71
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:27.778
Do not retry merely because the caller saw
a timeout.

72
00:08:27.778 --> 00:08:33.556
Correlate the exact request and operation,
query the external system

73
00:08:33.556 --> 00:08:39.333
of record, inspect the durable
postcondition, and determine whether the

74
00:08:39.333 --> 00:08:45.111
action completed, failed, or remains
uncertain. Only then choose no

75
00:08:45.111 --> 00:08:50.889
action, an idempotent or otherwise safe
retry, or an authorized

76
00:08:50.889 --> 00:08:56.667
compensating operation. Preserve the
reconciliation and decision without

77
00:08:56.667 --> 00:09:02.444
exposing unnecessary content. If your
answer retried immediately, it could

78
00:09:02.444 --> 00:09:08.222
duplicate a financial, administrative,
communication, or data-changing action.

79
00:09:08.222 --> 00:09:14.000
The safe sequence is reconcile, authorize,
act, and verify.

80
00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:20.733
Recover to explicit objectives. Choose a
tested action: remove traffic,

81
00:09:20.733 --> 00:09:27.467
restart one bounded component, replace a
corrupt artifact, rotate and

82
00:09:27.467 --> 00:09:34.200
revoke a secret, restore configuration or
durable state, scale within

83
00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:40.933
an evaluated envelope, fail over, roll
back the complete serving

84
00:09:40.933 --> 00:09:47.667
unit, or remain unavailable pending human
authority. Where durable state

85
00:09:47.667 --> 00:09:54.400
exists, state recovery-time and
recovery-point objectives and the evidence

86
00:09:54.400 --> 00:10:01.133
that proves them. Validate the complete
service contract: exact model

87
00:10:01.133 --> 00:10:07.867
and dependency identity, authentication
and authorization, representative quality

88
00:10:07.867 --> 00:10:14.600
and safety cases, endpoint compatibility,
capacity and queue state, telemetry

89
00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:21.333
and alerting, cost behavior, reconciled
external effects, and user-visible

90
00:10:21.333 --> 00:10:28.067
postconditions. Restore traffic in bounded
stages with an observation window

91
00:10:28.067 --> 00:10:34.800
and recurrence stop condition. A green
liveness endpoint proves only

92
00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:41.533
that one process can answer its health
check. It does

93
00:10:41.533 --> 00:10:48.267
not prove the correct model, safe access,
valid outcomes, cleared

94
00:10:48.267 --> 00:10:55.000
queues, consistent state, working
telemetry, or restored user service.

95
00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:01.769
Communicate what is confirmed. Each update
states time, known scope

96
00:11:01.769 --> 00:11:08.538
and impact, current containment, service
status, next decision, and expected

97
00:11:08.538 --> 00:11:15.308
update cadence. Follow organizational
privacy, legal, customer, contractual, and

98
00:11:15.308 --> 00:11:22.077
regulatory duties. Avoid unsupported
root-cause claims and never publish

99
00:11:22.077 --> 00:11:28.846
sensitive operational evidence. Close only
after impact has stopped, recovery

100
00:11:28.846 --> 00:11:35.615
postconditions pass, required stakeholders
are informed, temporary access and

101
00:11:35.615 --> 00:11:42.385
emergency controls are removed, and
follow-up work has owners and

102
00:11:42.385 --> 00:11:49.154
dates. Build a blameless timeline that
identifies contributing conditions rather

103
00:11:49.154 --> 00:11:55.923
than searching for one person to blame.
Add privacy-reviewed reproducing

104
00:11:55.923 --> 00:12:02.692
cases, strengthen identity, capacity,
telemetry, release, backup, or rollback

105
00:12:02.692 --> 00:12:09.462
controls, update the runbook, rehearse the
revised response, and measure

106
00:12:09.462 --> 00:12:16.231
recurrence. A resolved alert without
validated recovery and owned prevention

107
00:12:16.231 --> 00:12:23.000
is not incident closure.

108
00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:29.333
Now run the incident exercise. Use the
scenario combining a

109
00:12:29.333 --> 00:12:35.667
bad release, queue delay, artifact
identity mismatch, and uncertain

110
00:12:35.667 --> 00:12:42.000
user-visible effect. Declare severity,
roles, cadence, containment, and evidence

111
00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:48.333
boundaries. Build the boundary diagnostic
